## Starting state

There is NO explorer or frontend directory in this repository today. Verify this
first (ls the repo root). You are creating this component from nothing, so you
also choose its layout — see "Placement" below.

The existing sdk/ package is the reference for tooling style and conventions
(TypeScript, vitest, polkadot-js, ESM, bigint balances). Read sdk/package.json
and sdk/README.md before choosing your own setup, and stay consistent with it.

## Placement

Create the component at explorer/ in the repository root, as a self-contained
TypeScript project with its own package.json. Do not restructure existing
directories to accommodate it, and do not add it to any existing workspace
config unless the repo already defines one (check the root Cargo.toml/
package.json first — this repo is Cargo-centric, so a standalone Node project is
the low-risk choice).

## What to build — minimal, working, real

Chain connection: ws://127.0.0.1:9944 (stock substrate node). Three views:

  1. BLOCKS — a list of recent blocks (number, hash, timestamp, extrinsic count,
     author if available) and a detail view for one block by number or hash.
  2. EXTRINSICS — for a block, the decoded extrinsics: index, signer, pallet,
     call name, arguments, success/failure with the decoded dispatch error, and
     the events each extrinsic emitted.
  3. ACCOUNTS — for an address: free/reserved balance, nonce, and (when the
     account is a registered agent) its agent state: stake, rank, era escrow
     volume, unclaimed emissions. Read the real pallet storage for these; see
     pallets/agents and pallets/emissions for the item names.

Requirements:
  - Balances are u128 plancks (1 CMN = 10^12). Format for display, but NEVER
    parse or accumulate them as JS numbers — bigint or string end to end. Include
    a test with a value above 2^53 that would lose precision as a float.
  - Handle the disconnected case as a first-class state: if no node is listening,
    the UI must show a clear "cannot reach node" state, not a blank page, not a
    crash, and not fabricated data.
  - Decode with @polkadot/api metadata rather than hand-rolled parsing.

Keep it minimal. No auth, no charts, no styling framework beyond what you need,
no state-management library. Scope creep will be failed by the reviewer.

## Testing strategy — the gate must not need a live chain

You are writing the test suite as part of this task, from the behaviour above,
BEFORE the implementation:

  - Decoding/formatting tests over recorded or synthetic block, extrinsic, and
    account fixtures. These are the substance of the gate: they prove the views
    render real decoded data.
  - Component/render tests asserting the three views display the fields listed
    above from fixture data.
  - The disconnected-node state, asserted explicitly.
  - Balance formatting, including the >2^53 case.
  - Any live-node test must sit behind an env var (e.g. RUN_INTEGRATION=1) and be
    visibly skipped when unset, matching the pattern in sdk/tests.

Mocking the RPC transport is expected. Mocking your own decode/render logic is
not — the code under test must be the real path.

## THE GATE (the only definition of success)

    cd explorer && npm ci --no-audit --no-fund && npm run build && npm test

All three must pass. Define "build" and "test" scripts in package.json that do
real work (a build that compiles nothing, or a test script that runs no tests,
fails this task and the reviewer will check). Run the gate yourself before
finishing.

Commit in small scoped steps ("explorer: add extrinsic decoding + tests"). Do
not push and do not open a PR — the factory handles that.
